{
  "dom_id": "step-q127813",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Consider a vector-valued function $f(u)$ that is continuous over all real numbers and the real-valued function $u(t)=\\sqrt{3t+7}.$ Then, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to -1}f(u(t))=\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that $u(t)=\\sqrt{3t+7}$ is continuous at $t=-1$ and \n\n$$\nu(-1)=\\sqrt{3(-1)+7}=2.\n$$\n\nSince $f(u)$ is continuous at $u=2$ and $u(t)$ is continuous at $t=-1,$ the function $f(u(t))$ is also continuous at $t=-1.$\n\nTherefore,\n${lim}_{t\\to -1}f(u(t))=f(u(-1))=f(2).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
